1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,750
Does changing attack power and speed

2
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:05,840
automatically change how a character plays?

3
00:00:05,890 --> 00:00:09,110
We will examine combat and distinctive abilities in Rivals of Aether,

4
00:00:09,110 --> 00:00:12,190
then compare changes to stats and resources in Dungeons of Aether.

5
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:15,023
Starting with shared actions, we will work out what

6
00:00:15,023 --> 00:00:17,680
choices a character’s strengths and limitations create.

7
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:21,794
Both fighters move, jump, attack and travel between platforms.

8
00:00:21,794 --> 00:00:25,940
Their actions first establish that they are playing the same game.

9
00:00:26,090 --> 00:00:29,125
If every value is chosen independently, even the size

10
00:00:29,125 --> 00:00:32,331
of a difference becomes hard to judge. Begin with shared

11
00:00:32,331 --> 00:00:35,480
actions and the minimum performance needed to use them.

12
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:39,900
Then introduce differences. With the same attack button available, look at

13
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:43,960
where each character creates an opportunity and where they choose to move.

14
00:00:44,067 --> 00:00:46,983
Official gameplay shows different phenomena: fire

15
00:00:46,983 --> 00:00:50,315
persisting on an opponent, water and bubbles, and smoke.

16
00:00:50,315 --> 00:00:53,767
These differences go beyond small changes to attack power.

17
00:00:54,017 --> 00:00:56,834
Look at Zetterburn and Orcane in Rivals of Aether.

18
00:00:56,834 --> 00:00:59,989
Watching a burning opponent and watching the location of

19
00:00:59,989 --> 00:01:03,427
water direct the player’s attention to different information.

20
00:01:03,847 --> 00:01:07,282
Before tuning a distinctive rule, name what it changes:

21
00:01:07,282 --> 00:01:10,779
the opponent’s state, a location or an available action.

22
00:01:10,779 --> 00:01:13,527
Clear rules give numerical tuning a purpose.

23
00:01:14,250 --> 00:01:18,610
Forsburn’s footage shows two similar figures, followed by smoke obscuring

24
00:01:18,610 --> 00:01:22,910
the fighters. It changes where an opponent must look to make a decision.

25
00:01:23,050 --> 00:01:27,010
A damage table alone cannot describe this difference. Ask not only how

26
00:01:27,010 --> 00:01:31,140
strong an attack is, but also what judgment it demands from the opponent.

27
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:36,521
Smoke does not by itself prove invulnerability. Design and verify the

28
00:01:36,521 --> 00:01:41,530
appearance, actual interaction rules and available responses separately.

29
00:01:42,033 --> 00:01:46,692
The fighters travel across the ground, through the air and beyond the platform

30
00:01:46,692 --> 00:01:50,933
edge. Distance and position change the opportunities to use an ability.

31
00:01:51,033 --> 00:01:55,538
A strength needs more than a large number in a table. Connect it to

32
00:01:55,538 --> 00:02:00,043
an advantageous choice and to the means of reaching that situation.

33
00:02:06,323 --> 00:02:09,677
For a hypothetical close-pressure role, approach tools and

34
00:02:09,677 --> 00:02:12,858
choices after an attack belong together. Raising damage

35
00:02:12,858 --> 00:02:16,443
without any way to get close can leave that strength unusable.

36
00:02:16,717 --> 00:02:21,130
Once a strength exists, consider its limitations. Reducing every ability until

37
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:25,317
nothing works is different from giving a character a distinctive weakness.

38
00:02:25,547 --> 00:02:28,530
In a game with constantly changing positions, check

39
00:02:28,530 --> 00:02:31,631
whether a character can leave an unfavorable distance

40
00:02:31,631 --> 00:02:34,557
or return to a range where their tools are useful.

41
00:02:34,977 --> 00:02:37,985
Keep responses available to the opponent too.

42
00:02:37,985 --> 00:02:41,661
Readable preparation and openings after failure connect

43
00:02:41,661 --> 00:02:45,137
strengths and limitations into a coherent playstyle.

44
00:02:45,457 --> 00:02:48,928
Distinguish a playable character’s role from an enemy’s role too.

45
00:02:48,928 --> 00:02:52,400
In a hypothetical design, even a simple charging enemy can demand

46
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,818
a different response in a narrow corridor or in combination with

47
00:02:55,818 --> 00:02:59,557
another enemy. Verify the placement’s effect through actual playtests.

48
00:02:59,683 --> 00:03:03,823
The percentages at the bottom are not an attack-power table. They show

49
00:03:03,823 --> 00:03:07,963
current damage state, while remaining stocks are displayed separately.

50
00:03:08,243 --> 00:03:11,695
Separate base capabilities, active effects and player

51
00:03:11,695 --> 00:03:15,407
decisions when comparing characters. A high percentage in

52
00:03:15,407 --> 00:03:19,053
one moment cannot establish which character is stronger.

53
00:03:24,983 --> 00:03:29,796
This is historical match footage. We use it to observe actions and states,

54
00:03:29,796 --> 00:03:34,543
not to derive current character rankings or universal damage multipliers.

55
00:03:34,750 --> 00:03:39,273
Dice and abilities change combat values in Dungeons of Aether. Hamir’s

56
00:03:39,273 --> 00:03:43,990
REACT sequence shows a defense change followed by a blocked enemy attack.

57
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:47,799
Slade’s STEAL increases coins after connecting, while

58
00:03:47,799 --> 00:03:51,016
Artemis’s STUN affects the opponent’s dice state.

59
00:03:51,016 --> 00:03:54,430
These are separate shots, not one continuous battle.

60
00:03:54,710 --> 00:03:59,104
An ability’s identity is not limited to damage. Defense, resources and the

61
00:03:59,104 --> 00:04:03,676
opponent’s options can also define a role. The displayed dice values are used

62
00:04:03,676 --> 00:04:08,070
in the current turn; they are not the character’s fixed base capabilities.

63
00:04:08,083 --> 00:04:11,360
Fleet’s SNIPE description says that connecting deals damage

64
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:14,526
and grants two stamina dice on the next turn. It does not

65
00:04:14,526 --> 00:04:17,803
mean six stamina is added immediately to the current value.

66
00:04:18,053 --> 00:04:21,252
STRIKE in a separate boss shot shows a heart decreasing

67
00:04:21,252 --> 00:04:24,159
with the hit. Similar-looking attack effects still

68
00:04:24,159 --> 00:04:27,183
need separate descriptions of what changes and when.

69
00:04:27,373 --> 00:04:30,740
Write the trigger, affected target and application

70
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:34,444
time together. This helps the program’s actual behavior

71
00:04:34,444 --> 00:04:38,013
and the on-screen explanation describe the same rule.

72
00:04:38,017 --> 00:04:41,882
Differences alone do not make every combination fair. Making every capability

73
00:04:41,882 --> 00:04:45,497
average does not automatically preserve interesting distinctions either.

74
00:04:45,737 --> 00:04:49,466
Check for opponents or locations with no workable response, and for

75
00:04:49,466 --> 00:04:53,418
tools that solve every situation without preparation. Review conditions

76
00:04:53,418 --> 00:04:57,147
across repeated tests rather than declaring balance from one match.

77
00:05:00,187 --> 00:05:03,026
Repair problematic conditions and values while preserving

78
00:05:03,026 --> 00:05:05,567
what the character does well. After a change, check

79
00:05:05,567 --> 00:05:08,407
whether the role still fits the same concise description.

80
00:05:08,467 --> 00:05:13,080
More unique rules create more combinations to verify: an effect

81
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:17,767
ending, a turn changing and an interaction with another ability.

82
00:05:18,117 --> 00:05:22,166
When adding distinct phenomena such as fire, water or smoke, begin

83
00:05:22,166 --> 00:05:26,277
with a concise core rule rather than an endless list of exceptions.

84
00:05:26,277 --> 00:05:30,327
Explicit states and transitions make test questions more concrete.

85
00:05:36,497 --> 00:05:39,775
In what situation does this character gain an advantage, through

86
00:05:39,775 --> 00:05:43,104
what tool, and how can an opponent respond? A shared answer keeps

87
00:05:43,104 --> 00:05:46,177
abilities and numerical tuning moving in the same direction.

88
00:05:46,467 --> 00:05:49,319
A character’s identity becomes clear when numerical

89
00:05:49,319 --> 00:05:52,731
differences create different player choices. Build strengths,

90
00:05:52,731 --> 00:05:55,807
limitations and distinctive rules on a shared baseline.

91
00:06:01,507 --> 00:06:05,143
Separate base capabilities from current state, and specify the

92
00:06:05,143 --> 00:06:08,839
conditions and timing of effects. Repair unusable weaknesses or

93
00:06:08,839 --> 00:06:12,417
universally dominant strengths while keeping the role intact.

94
00:06:12,837 --> 00:06:16,159
Before filling out the next character’s stat table, write one

95
00:06:16,159 --> 00:06:19,917
sentence about the situation and choice you want players to remember.
